Backdrop: uniform warm ivory.
Subject: a single Earth-sized abstract geoid centered slightly left of the canvas center. Its surface is like a gently lumpy potato, with broad smooth bulges and depressions strongly exaggerated so that the deviation from an ideal ellipsoid is easy to see. This represents a gravitational equipotential surface, the level of a hypothetical ocean extended over the whole Earth; it is NOT terrain, continents, mountains or ocean waves. Show the low areas in calm blue, intermediate areas in muted green, and high areas in gentle yellow, all connected by smooth gradients. Restrained soft volume shading.
Superimpose a smooth reference ellipsoid at exactly the same center and approximately the same mean radius: draw only its thin precise light-gray outer circular/very slightly oblate elliptical outline, without fill. The reference outline should remain visibly readable across the colored surface and against the ivory wherever the geoid dips inside it; some bulges extend outside it. Make the outline subtle but discernible. No wireframe or latitude/longitude grid.
Composition: one overlapping comparison, not two separate globes. Entire object visible, generous margins. Leave the rightmost 30 percent largely empty ivory for later slide text. The object occupies roughly the middle-left 60 percent of the canvas.
Style: dignified, calm, precise scientific textbook plate, fine delicate edges, smooth matte color, no dramatic lighting.
Strict exclusions: no letters, Japanese or English text, numbers, labels, logos, watermark, legends, colorbars, arrows, ticks, axes, symbols, decorative marks, continents, geographic borders, stars, or additional objects.
```


